  "result": "I added `split(cents, parts)` to `shop/money.py` with tests in a new file, `tests/test_money.py`. The full suite passes: 11 tests, including the 5 new ones.\n\n- **How it works:** it divides `cents` evenly across `parts`, then gives one extra cent to each of the first elements until the leftover is used up. For example, `split(1000, 3)` returns `[334, 333, 333]`.\n- **Invalid input:** `parts` below 1 raises `ValueError`.\n- **Tests cover:** an even split, leftover cents going to the first elements, more parts than cents (`split(2, 4)` returns `[1, 1, 0, 0]`), a single part, and `parts` of 0 and -2.\n\nDecision for you: negative amounts aren't handled the way you'd probably expect. `split(-7, 3)` returns `[-2, -2, -3]`. That still adds up to -7, but the odd cent lands on the last element instead of the first. If refunds go through `split`, I can make negative amounts mirror positive ones (`[-3, -2, -2]`). I left it alone because you didn't say how negatives should behave.",
